# The read surfaces the LAN machine door carries, one `name=port` per
# line — the table boss prove --unattended hands a car's probe as
# BOSS_SOR_PORTS so `boss-sor-read /api/events/tail` reaches
# boss-events-api and not a 404 on the jobs port (design 28d2bed9,
# David 2026-09-17).
#
# WHY A FILE AND NOT A RENDER. The definition of every port is
# crates/core/boss-ports; the container launcher's roster is pinned to
# it through the boss-ports-list binary. The forge host has
# no cargo and no built binary — its checkout is a git tree and the
# ops-runner reads verbs from it — so this is the one place a table
# rendered from boss-ports can live for that host: in the tree, next to
# the reader, converging with every forge-converge tick and needing no
# unit reinstall. It is a copy, and a copy is pinned (CLAUDE.md §9a):
# crates/core/boss-testing/tests/the_machine_door_carries_every_read_surface.rs
# holds these lines, the door's manifest (boss-jobs-internal.yaml) and
# the reader's route table equal to boss-ports, and names the entry
# that drifts. To add a surface: a route in probe-bin/sor-routes.sh
# (the one table boss-sor-read AND the pod's infra/dev/boss-api source
# — which also reads THIS file, from beside itself, since backlog
# de0989d2), a port line in the manifest, a line here — the test
# refuses two of the three.
#
# Read as DATA by the unattended prove door, never sourced: the
# runner is root, and a file it executes would be a fourth thing to
# trust. `#` comments and blank lines are ignored.
jobs=7900
events=7150
people=7500
classes=7800
locations=7820
accounts=7550
ledger=7080
dispatcher=7950
